claimAssessments.reasoning:
The conventional account is strongly evidenced:
- standard-model skies reproduce the circle abundance (CCC-E003);
- size marginalization removes the Hawking-point excess (CCC-E010);
- few-pixel spots manufacture concentric sets (CCC-E012);
- a low-multipole mechanism accounts for large-radius deficits (CCC-E056);
- the framework fits the sky precisely (CCC-E020).
But the claim says it fully accounts for every signature. The ellipse preference and the specific centre concentration have never been tested against it, so complete accounting is provisional; this adopts the GPT and Opus dissents. CCC-E021 records that the model's tensor signature is still unseen.

Five independent reanalyses reproduce the rings and find them at chance abundance against standard-model skies:
- Wehus & Eriksen (CCC-E002);
- Moss, Scott & Zibin (CCC-E003);
- Hajian, with realistic noise (CCC-E004);
- Bielewicz et al., over 0–90° (CCC-E037);
- DeAbreu et al., with the proponents' own parameters on Planck (CCC-E008).
Bodnia et al. show few-pixel spots generate concentric sets (CCC-E012). The supporting records (CCC-E001, CCC-E006, CCC-E007) rest on disputed or post-hoc comparisons, and CCC-E023 shows neither side preregistered.
